<$fill $name="body">

!! What it does

Outline mode brings the Streams workflow to a normal tiddler. It is there in every wikitext tiddler, idle until you Ctrl-Click (Cmd-Click on Mac) a block. The tiddler renders as before; every block that comes from its own text becomes selectable. Select a block, edit it in place, move it, indent it. Each action rewrites the right part of that one tiddler's text.

!! How you use it

# Ctrl-Click (Cmd-Click on Mac) a block: the outline wakes and selects it. This works in wikitext tiddlers that are not drafts, system tiddlers, `code-body` or `hide-body` tiddlers, not left out with the field `outline: no` and not rendered by another plugin's body template. Until then plain clicks, double-clicks (word selection) and keys are TiddlyWiki's own. Escape with nothing selected, a click outside the tiddler or closing it puts the outline back to sleep (not while an editor is open); a closed tiddler forgets its selection and an open edit.
# Click a block or press Up/Down (Left/Right: parent / first child). A whole list counts as one block for Up/Down; Right enters it, Ctrl-Up/Down step through every item. A blue frame marks the selected unit (orange on a transclusion: its content belongs to another tiddler). A small badge in the right margin names each unit's type (paragraph, h2, code-block, bullet, ...), so heading levels and block types are easy to tell apart; the editor field carries the badge of what it edits. The settings switch it to headings only or off. On a list item it frames only the item's own line; moves still take its sub-items along.
# Shift-Up/Down (or Shift-Click) extend the selection to a range of neighbouring units at the same level; Escape or a plain Up/Down go back to one. Alt-Up/Down, Tab/Shift-Tab, Delete and Ctrl-C/X take the whole range (one undo step); //Externalize// in the block menu moves it into its own tiddler and leaves a transclusion.
# Alt-Up/Alt-Down move it (also across heading lines), Tab/Shift-Tab indent or promote it. Its subtree goes along. Alt-Enter inserts a block after or before it, Ctrl-Enter starts a new empty unit below it; an empty tiddler starts with Enter.
# Enter (or a double-click, also while another unit is edited: that one is saved) opens the editor in place, the caret at the end of the text: a compact one-line field (the full editor with toolbar is a setting). It shows the content only, without the list marker or heading `!`s, a code block's fence lines or the `>`s of a quote, so editing never changes the nesting. Ctrl-Enter saves, Enter saves and opens a new unit (at the start of a filled item or paragraph: an empty one above), Escape discards. Ctrl-Up/Down save and move the editor to the previous/next unit, Alt-Enter turns it into another type.
# Delete removes the selected unit with everything below it, after a yes/no menu; on a heading the menu also offers to delete just the heading line. In the editor, Enter in the middle of a list item splits it at the caret, Backspace at its start merges it into the item above and Delete at its end pulls the item below up. Ctrl-Z undoes any of these changes, Ctrl-Y redoes, in the editor too while nothing is typed. Ctrl-C / Ctrl-X / Ctrl-V copy, cut and paste a unit with its subtree through the system clipboard: formatted into an e-mail, or into another tiddler, where a paste takes the level of the place.
# Alt-Enter opens the block menu: insert a new block of a type after or before the selection, or turn the selection into another type; in the editor it turns the edited unit into another type, and in a new, still empty editor it changes what the new unit becomes. On a list item the menu also has //Add to this item//: a diagram (with rimir/graphviz or rimir/mermaid) becomes a tiddler of its own, transcluded in the item.
# On a transclusion line (or a bullet with a transclusion) Enter edits the line like any other unit; Shift-Enter opens the transcluded tiddler, already in outline mode. For a diagram (a transcluded tiddler of a registered type) Shift-Enter, or a double-click on the diagram, opens its source in place.
# `?` (or the small ? button at the top right) shows all keys next to the outline; Escape closes it. The //keys// tab has the same overview, the settings tab changes the keys.

!! Units

* List items (`*`, `#`, `;`, `:`, `>`) - the item's own lines; nested items are its subtree.
* Lists as a whole.
* Headings, with their section (up to the next heading of the same or a higher level) as subtree.
* Paragraphs, code blocks, tables.
* Plain HTML containers (`<div>`, `<section>`, ...) are opened: their inner blocks are units, and the container is one too.
* Transclusion lines (`{{...}}`, `<$transclude/>`).
* Widgets (`<$list>`, `<$reveal>`, ...) and macro or procedure calls: opaque, one unit each.

Pragmas (`\define`, `\procedure`, `\import`, ...) are never units. A unit that renders nothing cannot be selected.

!! Read-only wikis

With `$:/status/IsReadOnly` = yes, only navigation (selection, transclusion jump) works.

